win32u: no window frame at all, because nothing here draws one.

Vessel runs every session inside `explorer /desktop=`, with its own shell
owning the window list, the move and resize handles and the task bar. The
window manager Wine defers to decorates nothing, so nothing should be reserved
for it.

Two changes, and both are needed:

  * adjust_window_rect no longer insets the client by a caption or a resize
    border, so the client is the window. That function is what every consumer
    agrees through -- WM_NCCALCSIZE, the visible rect the X window is created
    from, and the AdjustWindowRect an application calls to size itself.

  * decorated_mode defaults to FALSE, so get_visible_rect stops shrinking the
    X window by the frame the window manager claims through
    X11DRV_GetWindowStyleMasks.

What this removes, measured on device with VS Code. Win32 said the client was
(360,140)-(1560,940) while the X window Vessel composites sat at (360,192),
1200x748 -- moved down 52 and shortened by 52, one caption plus border. Nobody
wrote those rows, so they kept whatever the surface held: white when freshly
allocated, black once a resize reallocated it, with the same 52 clipped off the
bottom. Removing the caption alone left a white ring in its place, for the same
reason one step smaller: the client stopped 11 pixels short on each side, the X
window now covered the whole window rect, and Chromium answers WM_NCPAINT
without drawing because it believes that ring belongs to the window manager.

Chromium was innocent throughout, and a DevTools capture is what proved it: VS
Code painted its own title bar, dark, at rows 0..34 of its 1200x800 surface,
into pixels the compositor then placed 52 rows low. That is why thirteen
Chromium switches, every VS Code title-bar setting and two Wine paint theories
all missed -- they were aimed at a renderer that was already correct.

Menu bars and non-client scroll bars keep their space: those are subtracted by
handle_nc_calc_size and by the `menu` argument, not here. That is what still
separates this from the gamescope early return a few lines above, which returns
before any of it is added and would cost an ordinary desktop program its scroll
bars. WS_EX_CLIENTEDGE is also left alone -- it is the sunken edge on ordinary
controls, which Wine itself paints, so it is not a source of unpainted pixels.

This does not duplicate patches/wine/0010, and the difference is the whole
reason the band survived a patch already written for it. 0010 clears
WS_CAPTION|WS_THICKFRAME in win32u s style-correction block at
NtUserCreateWindowEx, which is the only place that works for a program that
never touches its style again -- but it is creation time only. Chromium sets
WS_CAPTION back afterwards, deliberately, so that resize, snap and drag keep
working; its style reads 0x6CC0000 by the time a window is shown. 0010 removes
the style and can be undone; this removes the geometry and cannot.

Windows still move and resize: the shell owns those handles, WS_CAPTION stays
on the window, and hit testing, snapping and anything reading the style are
unchanged. Chromium in particular keeps WS_CAPTION deliberately for that.

HKCU\Software\Wine\X11 Driver\Decorated still overrides the second half, so a
session that really does have a decorating window manager can say so without a
rebuild.

--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -262,7 +262,6 @@
 BOOL adjust_window_rect( RECT *rect, DWORD style, BOOL menu, DWORD ex_style, UINT dpi )
 {
     NONCLIENTMETRICSW ncm = {.cbSize = sizeof(ncm)};
-    int adjust = 0;
 
     if (user_driver->pHasWindowManager( "steamcompmgr" ))
     {
@@ -282,26 +281,59 @@
 
     NtUserSystemParametersInfoForDpi( SPI_GETNONCLIENTMETRICS, 0, &ncm, 0, dpi );
 
-    if ((ex_style & (WS_EX_STATICEDGE|WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME)) == WS_EX_STATICEDGE)
-        adjust = 1; /* for the outer frame always present */
-    else if ((ex_style & WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME) || (style & (WS_THICKFRAME|WS_DLGFRAME)))
-        adjust = 2; /* outer */
-
-    if (style & WS_THICKFRAME)
-        adjust += ncm.iBorderWidth + ncm.iPaddedBorderWidth; /* The resize border */
-
-    if ((style & (WS_BORDER|WS_DLGFRAME)) || (ex_style & WS_EX_DLGMODALFRAME))
-        adjust++; /* The other border */
-
-    InflateRect( rect, adjust, adjust );
-
-    if ((style & WS_CAPTION) == WS_CAPTION)
-    {
-        if (ex_style & WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW)
-            rect->top -= ncm.iSmCaptionHeight + 1;
-        else
-            rect->top -= ncm.iCaptionHeight + 1;
-    }
+    /* Vessel: no resize border either, for the reason the caption goes below.
+     *
+     * The border is a decoration too, and leaving it in left a white ring
+     * around every Chromium window once the caption went: the client stops
+     * 11 pixels short of the window on each side, decorated_mode = FALSE means
+     * the X window now covers the whole window rect rather than just the
+     * client, and Chromium answers WM_NCPAINT without drawing -- it believes
+     * that ring is the window manager s. So the ring belonged to nobody and
+     * kept the surface fill, exactly as the caption band did before it.
+     *
+     * With this the client is the window, so win32u::get_visible_rect takes its
+     * EqualRect( window, client ) early return and the X window matches the
+     * surface by construction rather than by both being adjusted the same way.
+     *
+     * Menu bars and non-client scroll bars are unaffected: those are subtracted
+     * by handle_nc_calc_size and by the `menu` argument below, not here, which
+     * is what still separates this from upstream s gamescope early return.
+     */
+
+    /* Vessel: no window gets a caption, because nothing in this stack draws one.
+     *
+     * Vessel runs every session inside `explorer /desktop=`, with its own shell
+     * owning the window list, the move and resize handles and the task bar. A
+     * Win32 caption on top of that is a second title bar for the same window,
+     * and the reason this is removed here rather than hidden later is that the
+     * caption is not only drawn, it is *reserved*: adjust_window_rect is what
+     * insets the client, so every consumer of it -- WM_NCCALCSIZE, the visible
+     * rect the X window is created from, and the AdjustWindowRect an
+     * application calls to size itself -- agrees there is no caption at all.
+     *
+     * **Leaving it in cost a blank band across the top of every Chromium
+     * window.** Measured on device: VS Code client (360,140)-(1560,940) while
+     * the X window sat at (360,192) 1200x748 -- moved down 52 and shortened by
+     * 52, one caption plus border. win32u::get_visible_rect shrinks the X
+     * window by whatever frame the window manager claims to draw
+     * (X11DRV_GetWindowStyleMasks -> MWM_DECOR_TITLE), and Vessel s shell draws
+     * none of it, so those 52 rows were never written by anybody: white while
+     * the surface was fresh, black once a resize reallocated it, with the same
+     * 52 rows clipped off the bottom. Chromium was innocent -- a DevTools
+     * capture showed it painting its own title bar, dark, at rows 0..34 of its
+     * 1200x800 surface, into pixels the compositor then placed 52 rows low.
+     *
+     * Only the caption goes. The border, menu bar and non-client scroll bars
+     * below still get their space, which is why this is a few lines here rather
+     * than the early return upstream takes for gamescope a few lines above --
+     * that one returns before the frame is added at all, and Vessel runs
+     * ordinary desktop programs that would lose their scroll bars with it.
+     *
+     * Windows still resize and move: the shell owns those handles, and
+     * WS_CAPTION stays on the window, so hit testing, snapping and anything
+     * that reads the style are unchanged. Chromium in particular keeps
+     * WS_CAPTION deliberately for exactly that reason.
+     */
     if (menu) rect->top -= ncm.iMenuHeight + 1;
 
     if (ex_style & WS_EX_CLIENTEDGE)
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -170,7 +170,25 @@
 static BOOL emulate_modelist = TRUE;
 static BOOL emulate_modeset = TRUE;
 static UINT limit_resolutions = 0;
-BOOL decorated_mode = TRUE;
+/* Vessel: FALSE, because the window manager here decorates nothing.
+ *
+ * This flag is what makes win32u::get_visible_rect shrink the X window by the
+ * frame the window manager claims it will draw. Vessel s shell owns the window
+ * list, the move and resize handles and the task bar, and draws no frame at
+ * all, so every pixel reserved that way belongs to nobody and is never
+ * written: white while the surface is fresh, black once a resize reallocates
+ * it. Measured on device as a 52 row band across the top of every Chromium
+ * window, with the same 52 rows clipped off the bottom.
+ *
+ * The pair to the caption removal in defwnd.c adjust_window_rect: that one
+ * stops a caption being reserved and drawn, this one stops the X window being
+ * inset for a frame nobody paints. Both are needed -- with only the first, the
+ * border is still reserved and the band merely gets thinner.
+ *
+ * Still a registry key: HKCU\Software\Wine\X11 Driver\Decorated overrides
+ * it, so a session that really does have a decorating window manager can say
+ * so without a rebuild. */
+BOOL decorated_mode = FALSE;
 UINT64 thunk_lock_callback = 0;
 
 #define VIRTUAL_HMONITOR ((HMONITOR)(UINT_PTR)(0x10000 + 1))
